\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Left:} a random (not curated) sample of the real Quick, Draw! \emph  {training} drawings, inspected before training. Several are ambiguous even to a human --- these are 20-second public doodles --- which is what sets the $0.8375$ recognisability ceiling rather than $1.0$. \textbf  {Right:} the memorisation probe. Generated samples sit at mean $L_2$ $16.62$ from their nearest training drawing versus $15.69$ for real held-out drawings, and even the six closest pairs share only coarse layout, not strokes. \leavevmode {\color  {gray}\small  \texttt  {C\_real\_data\_grid.png}, \texttt  {C\_memorisation\_probe.png}}\relax }}{8}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:Cdata}{{5}{8}{\textbf {Left:} a random (not curated) sample of the real Quick, Draw! \emph {training} drawings, inspected before training. Several are ambiguous even to a human --- these are 20-second public doodles --- which is what sets the $0.8375$ recognisability ceiling rather than $1.0$. \textbf {Right:} the memorisation probe. Generated samples sit at mean $L_2$ $16.62$ from their nearest training drawing versus $15.69$ for real held-out drawings, and even the six closest pairs share only coarse layout, not strokes. \textcolor {gray}{\small \texttt {C\_real\_data\_grid.png}, \texttt {C\_memorisation\_probe.png}}\relax }{figure.caption.10}{}}
